tion of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=-2x+19.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-08d99b4e-ce04-5c53-b6e9-817819d842a6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-08d99b4e-ce04-5c53-b6e9-817819d842a6" :answer-field/id #uuid "08d99b4e-ce04-5c53-b6e9-817819d842a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7a00ca04-f14c-58d5-9ab3-c8221bd600cf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7a00ca04-f14c-58d5-9ab3-c8221bd600cf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a00ca04-f14c-58d5-9ab3-c8221bd600cf" :answer/id #uuid "7a00ca04-f14c-58d5-9ab3-c8221bd600cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2x+19" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-22d42a89-d817-5352-921e-6f3ec86ac62f" :question/id #uuid "22d42a89-d817-5352-921e-6f3ec86ac62f" :question/math-academy-id "q-134521" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $7$ | $5$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nConsider the data sets above. You are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=2$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=3.$ Find the equation of the corresponding linear regression line. Round the slope and the $y$-intercept to $1$ decimal place if necessary." :question/worked-solution "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(0-2)(7-3)+(1-2)(5-3) \\\\  & +(3-2)(1-3)+(4-2)(-1-3) \\\\  & =(-2)(4)+(-1)(2)+(1)(-2)+(2)(-4) \\\\  & =-8-2-2-8 \\\\  & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(0-2)^{2}+(1-2)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}+(4-2)^{2} \\\\  & =4+1+1+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=\\frac{-20}{10}=-2.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =3-(-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3+4 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=-2x+7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b5b4ca26-bcc7-56b7-afb9-4a67fc3172f9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b5b4ca26-bcc7-56b7-afb9-4a67fc3172f9" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b4ca26-bcc7-56b7-afb9-4a67fc3172f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ef6e8408-98d8-5ae0-8d49-775c9ff55469" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d0486f24-94ec-5707-918c-3131894abe26" "answer-37cbb3fd-73e5-5e8d-9690-8763071dfd32" "answer-45a9f338-b92d-5726-b985-633b4a3fbffd" "answer-ef6e8408-98d8-5ae0-8d49-775c9ff55469" "answer-4e825e05-cf1d-521e-8e48-f222ef8fea1f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0486f24-94ec-5707-918c-3131894abe26" :answer/id #uuid "d0486f24-94ec-5707-918c-3131894abe26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-1.2x+6.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37cbb3fd-73e5-5e8d-9690-8763071dfd32" :answer/id #uuid "37cbb3fd-73e5-5e8d-9690-8763071dfd32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-1.6x+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45a9f338-b92d-5726-b985-633b4a3fbffd" :answer/id #uuid "45a9f338-b92d-5726-b985-633b4a3fbffd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2.1x+7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef6e8408-98d8-5ae0-8d49-775c9ff55469" :answer/id #uuid "ef6e8408-98d8-5ae0-8d49-775c9ff55469" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2x+7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e825e05-cf1d-521e-8e48-f222ef8fea1f" :answer/id #uuid "4e825e05-cf1d-521e-8e48-f222ef8fea1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-7x+1.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cc8754de-7209-53e4-a8f7-9e3f115b2f92" :question/id #uuid "cc8754de-7209-53e4-a8f7-9e3f115b2f92" :question/math-academy-id "q-134589" :question/problem "| **Study Hours ($x$)** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$1$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Average Grade ($y$)** | $71$ | $75$ | $65$ | $85$ |\n\nThe table above gives the number of study hours per day and the corresponding average grades (in points) of four students. You are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=2$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=74.$ \n\nFind the equation of the corresponding linear regression line and use this to predict the average grade for a student that studies $3$ hours per day. Round the regression line's coefficients to three decimal places and your final answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(1-2)(71-74)+(2-2)(75-74) \\\\  & +(1-2)(65-74)+(4-2)(85-74) \\\\  & =(-1)(-3)+(0)(1)+(-1)(-9)+(2)(11) \\\\  & =3+0+9+22 \\\\  & =34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(1-2)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(1-2)^{2}+(4-2)^{2} \\\\  & =1+0+1+4 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=\\frac{34}{6}=\\frac{17}{3}≈5.667.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =74-\\frac{17}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{188}{3} \\\\  & ≈62.667\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=5.667x+62.667.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can predict the average grade of a student that studies $3$ hours per day by substituting $x=3$ into our regression line:\n\n$$\ny=5.667(3)+62.667≈80\n$$\n\nTherefore, the model predicts that a student that studies three hours per day will have an average grade of $80$ points." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-64e44a62-8213-5c6f-80df-59eef2307e74"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-64e44a62-8213-5c6f-80df-59eef2307e74" :answer-field/id #uuid "64e44a62-8213-5c6f-80df-59eef2307e74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-67c6c97a-0bb6-501a-b7cc-76baa2095830" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b64589e6-c3b6-5a58-adf0-488fce7b8549" "answer-0cd4c0fb-caf1-5f4f-a432-bb5fd3ff247b" "answer-d29d6acc-aa34-5c55-b6c6-cf266929ebe8" "answer-67c6c97a-0bb6-501a-b7cc-76baa2095830" "answer-de19434b-df45-5944-bc31-db1c8b80ded8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b64589e6-c3b6-5a58-adf0-488fce7b8549" :answer/id #uuid "b64589e6-c3b6-5a58-adf0-488fce7b8549" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$77$ points" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cd4c0fb-caf1-5f4f-a432-bb5fd3ff247b" :answer/id #uuid "0cd4c0fb-caf1-5f4f-a432-bb5fd3ff247b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$78$ points" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d29d6acc-aa34-5c55-b6c6-cf266929ebe8" :answer/id #uuid "d29d6acc-aa34-5c55-b6c6-cf266929ebe8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$79$ points" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67c6c97a-0bb6-501a-b7cc-76baa2095830" :answer/id #uuid "67c6c97a-0bb6-501a-b7cc-76baa2095830" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$80$ points" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de19434b-df45-5944-bc31-db1c8b80ded8" :answer/id #uuid "de19434b-df45-5944-bc31-db1c8b80ded8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$82$ points" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-92e8138f-b7f0-50e4-b13c-66a8af5c74ac" :question/id #uuid "92e8138f-b7f0-50e4-b13c-66a8af5c74ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-134594" :question/problem "| **Weight $(kg)$ of Apples ($x$)** | **$30$** | **$44$** | **$50$** | **$52$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Hours ($y$)** | $5$ | $6$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n\nA farmer wants to understand the relation between the weight of apples to harvest and the time needed to do the job.\nThe table above gives the weight of apples harvested and the corresponding number of work hours over a sample of four days. You are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=44$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=6.$ \n\nFind the equation of the corresponding linear regression line and use this to predict the time needed when there are $20$ kilograms of apples to collect. Round the regression line's coefficients to three decimal places and your final answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(30-44)(5-6)+(44-44)(6-6) \\\\  & +(50-44)(6-6)+(52-44)(7-6) \\\\  & =(-14)(-1)+(0)(0)+(6)(0)+(8)(1) \\\\  & =14+0+0+8 \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(30-44)^{2}+(44-44)^{2}+(50-44)^{2}+(52-44)^{2} \\\\  & =196+0+36+64 \\\\  & =296\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=\\frac{22}{296}=\\frac{11}{148}≈0.074.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{11}{148}\\cdot 44 \\\\  & ≈2.730\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=0.074x+2.730.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can predict the time needed to harvest $20$ kilograms by substituting $x=20$ into our regression line:\n\n$$\ny=0.074(20)+2.730≈4.2\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, it takes $4.2$ hours to harvest $20\\,\\text{kg}$ of apples.\n\nNote: Since we're extrapolating (the point where $x=20$ is not within the original data range), our result *may* be unreliable." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-52ec2b83-6910-5f4f-81eb-01603d3d75bc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-52ec2b83-6910-5f4f-81eb-01603d3d75bc" :answer-field/id #uuid "52ec2b83-6910-5f4f-81eb-01603d3d75bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b8bfc69b-143f-5630-830c-5683834f3ed2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e6cb6aa0-e154-57b0-8bed-8664f4ac1273" "answer-5717c494-07c0-5e56-a9d7-736aef722a2e" "answer-b8bfc69b-143f-5630-830c-5683834f3ed2" "answer-54cc76a5-4452-5793-ba72-32f997a468b7" "answer-28a0a6b5-fa56-5177-8252-49d5bec965c4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6cb6aa0-e154-57b0-8bed-8664f4ac1273" :answer/id #uuid "e6cb6aa0-e154-57b0-8bed-8664f4ac1273" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2.9$ hours" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5717c494-07c0-5e56-a9d7-736aef722a2e" :answer/id #uuid "5717c494-07c0-5e56-a9d7-736aef722a2e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3.5$ hours" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8bfc69b-143f-5630-830c-5683834f3ed2" :answer/id #uuid "b8bfc69b-143f-5630-830c-5683834f3ed2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4.2$ hours" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54cc76a5-4452-5793-ba72-32f997a468b7" :answer/id #uuid "54cc76a5-4452-5793-ba72-32f997a468b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$6.1$ hours" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28a0a6b5-fa56-5177-8252-49d5bec965c4" :answer/id #uuid "28a0a6b5-fa56-5177-8252-49d5bec965c4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$7.2$ hours" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f0e8a005-d5ef-567f-838c-cadbb31dd290" :question/id #uuid "f0e8a005-d5ef-567f-838c-cadbb31dd290" :question/math-academy-id "q-134604" :question/problem "| **Weeks ($x$)** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$7$** | **$8$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Increase in Words per Minute ($y$)** | $20$ | $44$ | $102$ | $118$ |\n\nThe instructor of a $10$-week speed reading course monitors the students' progress during the course.\nThe table above gives the number of weeks of attendance and the corresponding increase in the number of words per minute for four students. You are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=5$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=71.$ \n\nFind the equation of the corresponding linear regression line and use this to predict how many weeks are needed to see an increase of $50$ words per minute. Round the regression line's coefficients to three decimal places and your final answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(2-5)(20-71)+(3-5)(44-71) \\\\  & +(7-5)(102-71)+(8-5)(118-71) \\\\  & =(-3)(-51)+(-2)(-27)+(2)(31)+(3)(47) \\\\  & =153+54+62+141 \\\\  & =410\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(2-5)^{2}+(3-5)^{2}+(7-5)^{2}+(8-5)^{2} \\\\  & =9+4+4+9 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=\\frac{410}{26}=\\frac{205}{13}≈15.769.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =71-\\frac{205}{13}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-7.846\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=15.769x-7.846\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate the required value by substituting $y=50$ into the equation of the trend line and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =15.769x-7.846 \\\\ 50 & =15.769x-7.846 \\\\ 57.846 & =15.769x \\\\ x & ≈3.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, students have an increase of $50$ words per minute after approximately $3.7$ weeks." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2db85191-362a-53e4-8530-d74c2a65384d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2db85191-362a-53e4-8530-d74c2a65384d" :answer-field/id #uuid "2db85191-362a-53e4-8530-d74c2a65384d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-600b6b62-22db-55a2-aed2-2737121eeef9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-960d12df-8477-50bd-a1a0-3fd5f8923d11" "answer-e0690624-5ae0-5d48-bbe6-681f08580e14" "answer-600b6b62-22db-55a2-aed2-2737121eeef9" "answer-85ac7239-5b0a-5fb9-8dc3-9f474530606a" "answer-3fec5b9d-5c17-5896-a796-e52b199c8b66"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-960d12df-8477-50bd-a1a0-3fd5f8923d11" :answer/id #uuid "960d12df-8477-50bd-a1a0-3fd5f8923d11" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3.2$ weeks" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0690624-5ae0-5d48-bbe6-681f08580e14" :answer/id #uuid "e0690624-5ae0-5d48-bbe6-681f08580e14" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3.4$ weeks" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-600b6b62-22db-55a2-aed2-2737121eeef9" :answer/id #uuid "600b6b62-22db-55a2-aed2-2737121eeef9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3.7$ weeks" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85ac7239-5b0a-5fb9-8dc3-9f474530606a" :answer/id #uuid "85ac7239-5b0a-5fb9-8dc3-9f474530606a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4.0$ weeks" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fec5b9d-5c17-5896-a796-e52b199c8b66" :answer/id #uuid "3fec5b9d-5c17-5896-a796-e52b199c8b66" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4.5$ weeks" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8625effc-a6bf-5c01-9019-062227a65c85" :question/id #uuid "8625effc-a6bf-5c01-9019-062227a65c85" :question/math-academy-id "q-134605" :question/problem "| **Years ($x$)** | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Value ($y$)** | **$20$** | **$18$** | **$15$** | **$11$** |\n\nThe table above gives the value of a motorcycle a few years after it was purchased (in thousands of dollars). You are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=2$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=16.$ Find the equation of the corresponding linear regression line and use this to predict how long it takes for the motorcycle to have a value of $$8\\,000.$ Round the answer to the nearest year." :question/worked-solution "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(0-2)(20-16)+(1-2)(18-16) \\\\  & +(3-2)(15-16)+(4-2)(11-16) \\\\  & =(-2)(4)+(-1)(2)+(1)(-1)+(2)(-5) \\\\  & =-8-2-1-10 \\\\  & =-21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(0-2)^{2}+(1-2)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}+(4-2)^{2} \\\\  & =4+1+1+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{21}{10}=-2.1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =16-(-2.1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =20.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=-2.1x+20.2\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate the required value by substituting $y=8$ into the equation of the trend line and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2.1x+20.2 \\\\ 8 & =-2.1x+20.2 \\\\ -12.2 & =-2.1x \\\\ x & ≈6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, it takes $6$ years for the motorcycle to have a value of $$8\\,000.$\n\nNote: Since we're extrapolating (the point where $y=8$ is not within the original data range), our result *may* be unreliable." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f216e67f-ab1c-5fee-9211-3c0531cfbf93"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f216e67f-ab1c-5fee-9211-3c0531cfbf93" :answer-field/id #uuid "f216e67f-ab1c-5fee-9211-3c0531cfbf93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d3873d28-7cf2-58e7-b49f-e9b7810ef93e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cd85d822-b458-5afc-8f82-96c68776f968" "answer-ae54c441-d47e-508c-8999-640555d0f079" "answer-e1602e5e-50f0-5e4f-810a-60fa0c496f8c" "answer-d3873d28-7cf2-58e7-b49f-e9b7810ef93e" "answer-f67e0a68-39cc-5086-b355-1dee00b5c62c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd85d822-b458-5afc-8f82-96c68776f968" :answer/id #uuid "cd85d822-b458-5afc-8f82-96c68776f968" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$10$ years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae54c441-d47e-508c-8999-640555d0f079" :answer/id #uuid "ae54c441-d47e-508c-8999-640555d0f079" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1602e5e-50f0-5e4f-810a-60fa0c496f8c" :answer/id #uuid "e1602e5e-50f0-5e4f-810a-60fa0c496f8c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$5$ years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3873d28-7cf2-58e7-b49f-e9b7810ef93e" :answer/id #uuid "d3873d28-7cf2-58e7-b49f-e9b7810ef93e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$6$ years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f67e0a68-39cc-5086-b355-1dee00b5c62c" :answer/id #uuid "f67e0a68-39cc-5086-b355-1dee00b5c62c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$8$ years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097078 :question/problem "| **Age ($x$)** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Height ($y$)** | $95$ | $106$ | $110$ | $125$ |\n\nThe table above gives the ages, in years, and the corresponding heights, in centimeters, of Milly and Rasheed's four children. You are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=6$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=109.$ \n\nFind the equation of the corresponding linear regression line and use this to predict the children's height when they are $6$ years old. Round the regression line's coefficients to three decimal places and your final answer to the nearest tenth of a centimeter." :question/worked-solution "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(3-6)(95-109)+(5-6)(106-109) \\\\  & +(7-6)(110-109)+(9-6)(125-109) \\\\  & =(-3)(-14)+(-1)(-3)+(1)(1)+(3)(16) \\\\  & =42+3+1+48 \\\\  & =94\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(3-6)^{2}+(5-6)^{2}+(7-6)^{2}+(9-6)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+1+9 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=\\frac{94}{20}=\\frac{47}{10}=4.7.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =109-4.7\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =109-28.2 \\\\  & =80.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=4.7x+80.8.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can predict the height of a six-year-old child in this family by substituting $x=6$ into our regression line:\n\n$$\ny=4.7(6)+80.8=109\n$$\n\nTherefore, the model predicts that a six-year-old child will be $109.0\\,\\text{cm}$ tall." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186094441 :answer-field/correct "answer-83678f4e-e4ae-526f-84f4-1a82f18c8a00" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1e747479-8878-5613-8c9c-88451ac3c939" "answer-f5b789f3-1f71-5341-be9a-36e79910b938" "answer-0336c168-4099-510b-a594-3b3934526e38" "answer-77d80668-ccd3-5a96-a0d9-977ec6a4f2a0" "answer-83678f4e-e4ae-526f-84f4-1a82f18c8a00"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e747479-8878-5613-8c9c-88451ac3c939" :answer/id #uuid "1e747479-8878-5613-8c9c-88451ac3c939" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "107.7\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5b789f3-1f71-5341-be9a-36e79910b938" :answer/id #uuid "f5b789f3-1f71-5341-be9a-36e79910b938" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "107.9\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0336c168-4099-510b-a594-3b3934526e38" :answer/id #uuid "0336c168-4099-510b-a594-3b3934526e38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "108.5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77d80668-ccd3-5a96-a0d9-977ec6a4f2a0" :answer/id #uuid "77d80668-ccd3-5a96-a0d9-977ec6a4f2a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "108.6\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83678f4e-e4ae-526f-84f4-1a82f18c8a00" :answer/id #uuid "83678f4e-e4ae-526f-84f4-1a82f18c8a00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "109.0\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097892 :tutorial/content "For a set of $n$ paired observations $x_i$ and $y_i,$ the trend line is the straight line that best fits the data.\n\n![](images/a2/a23a1411b40f723b19446d0838346dd74dd7bf75da918859cc688171e808b44a.png)\n\nFinding the equation of the trend line is called **linear regression**. The trend line is commonly called the **regression line** or **line of best fit.**\n\nWe can show that the equation of the trend line is given by\n\n$$\ny = mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- The slope $m$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $y$-intercept $b$ is given by\n\n$$\nb = \\overline{y} - m \\overline{x}.\n$$\n\n- $\\overline{x}$ and $\\overline{y}$ are the means of the data sets:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} =  \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i, \\qquad \\overline{y} =  \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n y_i\n$$\n\n- The quantities $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$ are given by\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \n\\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y}).\n$$\n\nThere's some nice intuition behind the formula for $b.$ The trend line *always* passes through the point $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y}).$ Therefore, this point must satisfy the trend line equation $y = mx+b.$ We can then solve this equation for $b.$\n\nAlso, notice that $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$ are the summation terms that feature in the formulas for the variance and covariance, respectively." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096859 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147611 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-47178fa5-4a8f-586f-b7df-db62566e23eb" "question-9a590bc2-106a-5fb6-90ee-78ac8a96929f" "question-b9f71f42-cce6-5b97-9247-c6bd081ec3db"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147290 :question/worked-solution "The polyhedron is a **heptagonal prism** since it has two identical heptagonal faces connected by equal parallel line segments.\n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not an octagonal (pentagonal or hexagonal) prism since it does not have an octagonal (pentagonal or hexagonal)  base.\n\n- The solid is not a cube since not all of its faces are squares." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147615 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7e00fa35-b4fc-5e3e-94db-29044e83ec94" "question-d5776870-d936-568a-a9d6-0ec5e35815ef"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147294 :question/problem "![](images/be/beed0799de4f42cde58bf0c85f31a1a6850ef1227ea2827e9819a4edeb3ede4a.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the polyhedron shown above?\n\nI. Hexagonal prism\n\nII. Hexagonal pyramid\n\nIII. Rectangular prism" :question/worked-solution "The solid is a **hexagonal pyramid** since its base is a hexagon and all other $6$ faces meet at a single point\n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not a hexagonal prism since it doesn't have two identical hexagonal faces connected by equal parallel lines.\n\n- The solid is not a rectangular prism since not all of its faces are rectangles." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147455 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-92a0b96a-db69-5957-b993-54818494d7c0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147392 :question/worked-solution "The solid is a **right cone**. \n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The solid has one circular base and one vertex. So, it's a cone.\n\n- The axis that passes through the center of the circle and the vertex is perpendicular to the base. So, it's right." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147612 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8ad29a00-337d-58d4-9a53-85c6c404f218" "question-e13fd4e8-a6eb-59e1-8b7a-96e64d918763"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-47178fa5-4a8f-586f-b7df-db62566e23eb" :question/id #uuid "47178fa5-4a8f-586f-b7df-db62566e23eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-107239" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b892210357f67b41acf4e300f6ec4bc9e78a7c78ceda3c169105c33f02db7f8e.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the polyhedron shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The polyhedron is a **pentagonal prism** since it has two identical pentagonal faces connected by equal parallel line segments.\n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not a pyramid since a pyramid must have triangular faces (except, possibly, for the base) that meet at a single point.\n\n- The solid is not a rectangular prism since not all of its faces are rectangles." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-97edbf88-a069-529b-b559-67fe372edd6f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-97edbf88-a069-529b-b559-67fe372edd6f" :answer-field/id #uuid "97edbf88-a069-529b-b559-67fe372edd6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-11276600-b864-5e1e-962a-2676b950c823" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5f10050b-43c5-5c36-a2b2-31d75d77a6e3" "answer-11276600-b864-5e1e-962a-2676b950c823" "answer-3582502d-6f02-581a-b987-21140ebbff04" "answer-862e7a19-db8c-5988-9230-a18f5a1dcf00" "answer-503b0f9c-a554-5ee4-88f7-56bd846bd696"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f10050b-43c5-5c36-a2b2-31d75d77a6e3" :answer/id #uuid "5f10050b-43c5-5c36-a2b2-31d75d77a6e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Cube" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11276600-b864-5e1e-962a-2676b950c823" :answer/id #uuid "11276600-b864-5e1e-962a-2676b950c823" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Pentagonal prism" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3582502d-6f02-581a-b987-21140ebbff04" :answer/id #uuid "3582502d-6f02-581a-b987-21140ebbff04" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Pentagonal pyramid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-862e7a19-db8c-5988-9230-a18f5a1dcf00" :answer/id #uuid "862e7a19-db8c-5988-9230-a18f5a1dcf00" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Rectangular prism" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-503b0f9c-a554-5ee4-88f7-56bd846bd696" :answer/id #uuid "503b0f9c-a554-5ee4-88f7-56bd846bd696" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Rectangular pyramid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9a590bc2-106a-5fb6-90ee-78ac8a96929f" :question/id #uuid "9a590bc2-106a-5fb6-90ee-78ac8a96929f" :question/math-academy-id "q-61658" :question/problem "![](images/06/063c18aee27eba87852117748b3f90bf75a9785b466516cbf88c01f4300f94e9.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the polyhedron shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The polyhedron is a **pentagonal prism** since it has two identical pentagonal faces connected by equal parallel line segments.\n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not a pyramid since a pyramid must have triangular faces (except, possibly, for the base) that meet at a single point.\n\n- The solid is not a rectangular prism since not all of its faces are rectangles. Due to the same reasons, the solid is not a cube." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-22f786a6-e78e-5736-910e-6288975ecbb3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-22f786a6-e78e-5736-910e-6288975ecbb3" :answer-field/id #uuid "22f786a6-e78e-5736-910e-6288975ecbb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1bcd57cb-5a6c-5fb7-958b-036b8957aafc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8a7da3ac-4b50-5a93-af6c-eb3cd197b5fb" "answer-1bcd57cb-5a6c-5fb7-958b-036b8957aafc" "answer-4a094c6f-f5ca-5e0d-a8a9-8e80cbd3f6d5" "answer-9f3bbabf-305a-54f4-9a13-af9829719997" "answer-67155834-2da7-5205-a1da-e76c6b4dc19f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a7da3ac-4b50-5a93-af6c-eb3cd197b5fb" :answer/id #uuid "8a7da3ac-4b50-5a93-af6c-eb3cd197b5fb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Cube" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bcd57cb-5a6c-5fb7-958b-036b8957aafc" :answer/id #uuid "1bcd57cb-5a6c-5fb7-958b-036b8957aafc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Pentagonal prism" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a094c6f-f5ca-5e0d-a8a9-8e80cbd3f6d5" :answer/id #uuid "4a094c6f-f5ca-5e0d-a8a9-8e80cbd3f6d5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Pentagonal pyramid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f3bbabf-305a-54f4-9a13-af9829719997" :answer/id #uuid "9f3bbabf-305a-54f4-9a13-af9829719997" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Rectangular prism" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67155834-2da7-5205-a1da-e76c6b4dc19f" :answer/id #uuid "67155834-2da7-5205-a1da-e76c6b4dc19f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Triangular pyramid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147891 :question/worked-solution "The solid is a **rectangular prism** since all its faces are rectangles.\n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not a triangular (pentagonal or hexagonal) prism since it does not have a triangular (pentagonal or hexagonal) base.\n\n- The solid is not a cube since its faces are not squares." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186145100 :answer-field/correct 17592186145102 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-b9f71f42-cce6-5b97-9247-c6bd081ec3db" :question/id #uuid "b9f71f42-cce6-5b97-9247-c6bd081ec3db" :question/math-academy-id "q-61643" :question/problem "![](images/71/71c7fda05aab2b4d91df47361b0bbf58f3e65d383a7c4af4e05d27d3e7ea9147.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the polyhedron shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The polyhedron is a **cube** since all its faces are squares.\n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not a pyramid since a pyramid must have triangular faces (except, possibly, for the base).\n\n- The solid is not a triangular prism because it does not have two triangular bases connected by parallel lines." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-61f696f8-268a-5b8a-9f94-89ea6bbbe502"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-61f696f8-268a-5b8a-9f94-89ea6bbbe502" :answer-field/id #uuid "61f696f8-268a-5b8a-9f94-89ea6bbbe502" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5a869980-6012-5244-841a-4a65c259a4a5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5a869980-6012-5244-841a-4a65c259a4a5" "answer-d384c22d-d602-57db-943e-0615fbe06585" "answer-11b5cda0-8df3-5aff-a584-01d2bfa412ca" "answer-a774c8a8-cf5b-5175-865d-5be90b270abd" "answer-e6965e4d-509f-5c69-a4b7-1d491736baf6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a869980-6012-5244-841a-4a65c259a4a5" :answer/id #uuid "5a869980-6012-5244-841a-4a65c259a4a5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Cube" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d384c22d-d602-57db-943e-0615fbe06585" :answer/id #uuid "d384c22d-d602-57db-943e-0615fbe06585" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Pyramid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11b5cda0-8df3-5aff-a584-01d2bfa412ca" :answer/id #uuid "11b5cda0-8df3-5aff-a584-01d2bfa412ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Rectangular pyramid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a774c8a8-cf5b-5175-865d-5be90b270abd" :answer/id #uuid "a774c8a8-cf5b-5175-865d-5be90b270abd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Triangular prism" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6965e4d-509f-5c69-a4b7-1d491736baf6" :answer/id #uuid "e6965e4d-509f-5c69-a4b7-1d491736baf6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Triangular pyramid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7e00fa35-b4fc-5e3e-94db-29044e83ec94" :question/id #uuid "7e00fa35-b4fc-5e3e-94db-29044e83ec94" :question/math-academy-id "q-93196" :question/problem "![](images/90/90bdf56a0c09a2db4fae7b19d3625360c4fc148b8807cc3e8a8ba35d1bdf78bb.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the polyhedron shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The solid is a **heptagonal pyramid** since it is a pyramid whose base is a heptagon.\n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not a heptagonal prism since it has only one heptagonal face.\n\n- The solid is not a hexagonal pyramid since it has no hexagonal face.\n\n- The solid is not a rectangular prism since not all of its faces are rectangles. Due to the same reasons, the solid is not a cube." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9c511c8e-ae32-5826-89f3-c8b44c59f5ed"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9c511c8e-ae32-5826-89f3-c8b44c59f5ed" :answer-field/id #uuid "9c511c8e-ae32-5826-89f3-c8b44c59f5ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fe4abc1a-5fd5-54fe-8b11-a2c8da5fd9b0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8a9580ff-5149-5172-8c90-ad5914bd3fc0" "answer-37d49e79-adee-5d48-9f3c-54a923fdadac" "answer-fe4abc1a-5fd5-54fe-8b11-a2c8da5fd9b0" "answer-240a84fc-5e49-5666-9e47-92d9e5ca7720" "answer-f3c5fa83-39dd-5069-84f7-e554e4a0624c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a9580ff-5149-5172-8c90-ad5914bd3fc0" :answer/id #uuid "8a9580ff-5149-5172-8c90-ad5914bd3fc0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Cube" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37d49e79-adee-5d48-9f3c-54a923fdadac" :answer/id #uuid "37d49e79-adee-5d48-9f3c-54a923fdadac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Heptagonal prism" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe4abc1a-5fd5-54fe-8b11-a2c8da5fd9b0" :answer/id #uuid "fe4abc1a-5fd5-54fe-8b11-a2c8da5fd9b0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Heptagonal pyramid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-240a84fc-5e49-5666-9e47-92d9e5ca7720" :answer/id #uuid "240a84fc-5e49-5666-9e47-92d9e5ca7720" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Hexagonal pyramid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3c5fa83-39dd-5069-84f7-e554e4a0624c" :answer/id #uuid "f3c5fa83-39dd-5069-84f7-e554e4a0624c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Rectangular prism" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d5776870-d936-568a-a9d6-0ec5e35815ef" :question/id #uuid "d5776870-d936-568a-a9d6-0ec5e35815ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-69864" :question/problem "![](images/81/8144eea23c9f47796eb414502c73044582a3c417abd44684bc4a5cd12d56cfcc.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the polyhedron shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The polyhedron is a **hexagonal pyramid** since it is a pyramid whose base is a hexagon.  \n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not a rectangular pyramid because it does not have a rectangle as its base.\n\n- The solid is not a triangular pyramid because not all of its faces are triangles.\n\n- The solid is not a hexagonal prism since it has only one hexagonal face.\n\n- The solid is not a rectangular prism since not all of its faces are rectangles." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a97ab0d9-e503-5570-a4f3-0853a9b06c6d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a97ab0d9-e503-5570-a4f3-0853a9b06c6d" :answer-field/id #uuid "a97ab0d9-e503-5570-a4f3-0853a9b06c6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-639168a2-06ab-5640-ace4-2d4b667aaee9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-84065c86-6a7f-5133-88ea-2220ca5be17a" "answer-639168a2-06ab-5640-ace4-2d4b667aaee9" "answer-3d5f18e5-7640-5be2-abd6-035520eda7e2" "answer-26b08f80-9cfa-5b25-bc8d-851e83975bb4" "answer-1852fd6c-1599-5811-a563-a92f67931581"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84065c86-6a7f-5133-88ea-2220ca5be17a" :answer/id #uuid "84065c86-6a7f-5133-88ea-2220ca5be17a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Hexagonal prism" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-639168a2-06ab-5640-ace4-2d4b667aaee9" :answer/id #uuid "639168a2-06ab-5640-ace4-2d4b667aaee9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Hexagonal pyramid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d5f18e5-7640-5be2-abd6-035520eda7e2" :answer/id #uuid "3d5f18e5-7640-5be2-abd6-035520eda7e2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Rectangular prism" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26b08f80-9cfa-5b25-bc8d-851e83975bb4" :answer/id #uuid "26b08f80-9cfa-5b25-bc8d-851e83975bb4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Rectangular pyramid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1852fd6c-1599-5811-a563-a92f67931581" :answer/id #uuid "1852fd6c-1599-5811-a563-a92f67931581" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Triangular pyramid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147727 :question/worked-solution "The solid is an **oblique cylinder**. \n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The solid has two parallel circular bases. So, it's a cylinder.\n\n- The axis that passes through the centers of the circles is not perpendicular to the bases. So, it's oblique, not right." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186144125 :answer-field/correct 17592186144127 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-92a0b96a-db69-5957-b993-54818494d7c0" :question/id #uuid "92a0b96a-db69-5957-b993-54818494d7c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-117677" :question/problem "![](images/72/729850f1c057d9369a12bec3f1ef006b3c2c097ba6c528fdffe56ccee9eb499a.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the solid shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The solid is an **oblique cone**. \n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The solid has one circular base and one vertex. So, it's a cone.\n\n- The axis that passes through the center of the circle and the vertex is not perpendicular to the base. So, it's oblique, not right." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-baba818e-5aa7-5224-825e-4048b27eb804"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-baba818e-5aa7-5224-825e-4048b27eb804" :answer-field/id #uuid "baba818e-5aa7-5224-825e-4048b27eb804" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fd8faac2-9657-5de3-841f-7305f0805dce" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8609c6fc-3413-57d4-bcb0-0d0e2a7e81bb" "answer-fd8faac2-9657-5de3-841f-7305f0805dce" "answer-4a84f87b-e7d9-5221-a52c-d4b89fc62f04" "answer-c79c78d1-8f52-5aa4-9c17-b7967d2c09da" "answer-2826c6c4-02b2-5a89-96b6-6b8dd7fb8cfe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8609c6fc-3413-57d4-bcb0-0d0e2a7e81bb" :answer/id #uuid "8609c6fc-3413-57d4-bcb0-0d0e2a7e81bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Hemisphere" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd8faac2-9657-5de3-841f-7305f0805dce" :answer/id #uuid "fd8faac2-9657-5de3-841f-7305f0805dce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Oblique cone" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a84f87b-e7d9-5221-a52c-d4b89fc62f04" :answer/id #uuid "4a84f87b-e7d9-5221-a52c-d4b89fc62f04" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Oblique cylinder" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c79c78d1-8f52-5aa4-9c17-b7967d2c09da" :answer/id #uuid "c79c78d1-8f52-5aa4-9c17-b7967d2c09da" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Right cone" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2826c6c4-02b2-5a89-96b6-6b8dd7fb8cfe" :answer/id #uuid "2826c6c4-02b2-5a89-96b6-6b8dd7fb8cfe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Right cylinder" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8ad29a00-337d-58d4-9a53-85c6c404f218" :question/id #uuid "8ad29a00-337d-58d4-9a53-85c6c404f218" :question/math-academy-id "q-117720" :question/problem "Which of the following is a polyhedron?" :question/worked-solution "Each face in a polyhedron must be a polygon. Therefore, the only option that is a polyhedron is:\n\n![](images/1a/1a04515db528b35af4da3e164522ff54458cba3dee0562e361388860890aa7a7.png)\n\nNotice that all other shapes have at least one face which is not a polygon." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4a24d8be-b6f4-5a86-9f10-12282c04ffec"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4a24d8be-b6f4-5a86-9f10-12282c04ffec" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a24d8be-b6f4-5a86-9f10-12282c04ffec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-be9cc850-c904-5412-ad77-ef309042b047" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b9c5dcf2-7189-5973-8c16-0464dbcc0665" "answer-be9cc850-c904-5412-ad77-ef309042b047" "answer-6a9b1a6d-b367-5e8e-a08e-4698d053980a" "answer-fbcd0247-aacf-58f9-9d79-3baba37a7cad" "answer-b357d1a4-c557-5cbe-8d81-f549c2284e04"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9c5dcf2-7189-5973-8c16-0464dbcc0665" :answer/id #uuid "b9c5dcf2-7189-5973-8c16-0464dbcc0665" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/0b/0b1b4aac5d04861de198cd6991b03ea39b3c93ed8610c792434f6acaae8897a0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be9cc850-c904-5412-ad77-ef309042b047" :answer/id #uuid "be9cc850-c904-5412-ad77-ef309042b047" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1a/1a04515db528b35af4da3e164522ff54458cba3dee0562e361388860890aa7a7.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a9b1a6d-b367-5e8e-a08e-4698d053980a" :answer/id #uuid "6a9b1a6d-b367-5e8e-a08e-4698d053980a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/3c/3cebf028a99d03f7eb8b4bf2b4180c9910775f398cc615ae1b14a5cc2d9d798d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbcd0247-aacf-58f9-9d79-3baba37a7cad" :answer/id #uuid "fbcd0247-aacf-58f9-9d79-3baba37a7cad" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/e3/e37aea980c64fa049f01ca93bde55dce81a5ccf79c9dd0f9eb89b68ceb2ab87b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b357d1a4-c557-5cbe-8d81-f549c2284e04" :answer/id #uuid "b357d1a4-c557-5cbe-8d81-f549c2284e04" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/ea/ea810f28207774067f82126c838605549b9c57e508046f0df3d378c570452a50.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e13fd4e8-a6eb-59e1-8b7a-96e64d918763" :question/id #uuid "e13fd4e8-a6eb-59e1-8b7a-96e64d918763" :question/math-academy-id "q-93200" :question/problem "Which of the following is **not** a polyhedron?" :question/worked-solution "Each face in a polyhedron must be a polygon. Therefore, the only option that is **not** a polyhedron is:\n\n![](images/c3/c392851dd589a891946cbbb17f27164b96c7649abd1f54fbe2a4a943d97e382b.png)\n\nNotice that the top face is not a polygon (it has curved sides)." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6f8dc06d-21de-51ee-b331-74ea1a18eb50"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6f8dc06d-21de-51ee-b331-74ea1a18eb50" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f8dc06d-21de-51ee-b331-74ea1a18eb50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c2caddaa-7d98-50c6-a8f7-8b9a5e85d26c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3ef3ff53-5943-5089-89d1-279891d7d7c8" "answer-22e1f78b-0771-595e-b5bb-efe703b032f9" "answer-289cc259-a1fd-5e09-bdf8-c6632abb135a" "answer-c2caddaa-7d98-50c6-a8f7-8b9a5e85d26c" "answer-41cdce87-367f-5a43-a332-a54e98990a24"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ef3ff53-5943-5089-89d1-279891d7d7c8" :answer/id #uuid "3ef3ff53-5943-5089-89d1-279891d7d7c8" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/2e/2ef8e272dc3a650b83322a1b8f80d2691a3ac7406738e957daf819cb7317d926.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22e1f78b-0771-595e-b5bb-efe703b032f9" :answer/id #uuid "22e1f78b-0771-595e-b5bb-efe703b032f9" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/4e/4e0550f18e27ad9326b5606f23b502ff6879d2149f5bac5e2fcc829506748f0b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-289cc259-a1fd-5e09-bdf8-c6632abb135a" :answer/id #uuid "289cc259-a1fd-5e09-bdf8-c6632abb135a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/93/931007418b0e7f9af99bb34831fbd50119ab5d0468e0039405bb84cb33fbe9fe.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2caddaa-7d98-50c6-a8f7-8b9a5e85d26c" :answer/id #uuid "c2caddaa-7d98-50c6-a8f7-8b9a5e85d26c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/c3/c392851dd589a891946cbbb17f27164b96c7649abd1f54fbe2a4a943d97e382b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41cdce87-367f-5a43-a332-a54e98990a24" :answer/id #uuid "41cdce87-367f-5a43-a332-a54e98990a24" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/e1/e1640f2e0a73058da6c112fb63a268edf0ecfe2cf4d92bb8feec34590b58ec2b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148714 :tutorial/content "A **polyhedron** is a $3$-dimensional shape where each side is a polygon. These flat polygonal sides are called **faces**.\n\n**Prisms** are polyhedrons made of two identical polygonal faces (called **bases**) connected by equal parallel line segments. If these line segments are perpendicular to the bases, we have a **right** prism.\n\nLet's take a look at some common prisms.\n\n- A **triangular (right) prism** consists of two identical triangular faces connected by equal parallel line segments.\n\n![](images/95/9563bd4f9c40ff5921998ed5840f8e5b43fb1af579a64a06a14782be4a86ece2.png)\n\n- A **rectangular (right) prism** consists of two identical rectangular faces connected by equal parallel line segments. Notice that each face is a rectangle.\n\n![](images/39/39ea3dd56722c7fb05ba275a054ff46479e3ee96851dafdd66fe09455a517f1a.png)\n\n- A **cube** is a special case of a rectangular prism that consists of six equal square faces.\n\n![](images/41/415d03a9a886793eee4b4d16e50af26002c0881279481b2e70773b8b7bff02a5.png)\n\nBy adding more sides to the polygonal base, we can make more prisms. For example, the following shape is a pentagonal prism.\n\n![](images/8f/8f4dd8fe8c120eabbe5a1ad6413f6f0cd9cd3d22efe8b13e25ac3d2a916f3258.png)\n\n \n\nWe can also construct hexagonal prisms, heptagonal prisms, etc." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148718 :tutorial/content "**Pyramids** are polyhedrons made of a polygonal base, with the other triangular faces meeting at a single point. \n\nLet's take a look at some common pyramids.\n\n- A **triangular pyramid** has a triangular base with $3$ other triangular faces that meet at a single point.\n\n![](images/d8/d8597d1e526e2af72255f7dce1cc13e01cf76f1c53522003b126be67a0a6c55d.png)\n\n- A **rectangular pyramid** has a rectangular base with $4$ triangular faces that meet at a single point. If the base is a square, we call this a **square pyramid.**\n\n![](images/26/26700acaf9eae64227988ddcfb43269c0eba6f6447229ec8854cc1ebfeab3334.png)\n\n- A **pentagonal pyramid** has a pentagonal base with $5$ triangular faces that meet at a single point.\n\n![](images/4c/4c73b984b3e4a2ccb0796646ebb4c547947fab7359914599d928a63819818725.png)\n\nSimilarly to prisms, we can add more sides to the polygonal base to make more types of pyramids." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148719 :tutorial/content "Not all three-dimensional shapes are polyhedrons. Let's see some examples.\n\n- A ** sphere** is the set of points with the same distance to a fixed point called the **center.** A sphere is a $3$-dimensional analog of a circle. A ** hemisphere ** is half of a sphere.\n\n![](images/84/84e7b63b5ec8761d4a3ac8a00b15a08f0dab7d4f95a536721df0c82e2999873f.png)\n\n- A **cylinder** is a solid shape with two parallel circular bases. There are two types of cylinders.\n\nA cylinder is **right** if the axis that passes through the centers of the circular bases is perpendicular to the bases. Otherwise, the cylinder is **oblique.**\n\n![](images/f8/f8ddb161d2d8628dd1294cc0d0042437300787217b518be73f010e79be90dcf7.png)\n\n- A **cone** is a solid shape with one circular base and one vertex called the **apex**. There are two types of cones.\n\nA cone is **right** if the axis that passes through the center of the circular base and the vertex is perpendicular to the base. Otherwise, the cone is **oblique.**\n\n![](images/2f/2f9e9ca18a36336ed99695bb51b167ffbd83f002139728f8dfe15227bf9f7745.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147667 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229171 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-137d2706-a3fe-517c-a8b7-db4dd05fae28" "question-da40e310-ec0b-5b91-ae08-d59c190a3606" "question-e21a8112-0441-5b7a-9e9e-a86738fe8b02"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228946 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}-3y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(2t-\\pi )\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-3L\\{y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(2t-\\pi )\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $\\mathcal L\\left\\{y' \\right\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y' \\right\\}  = sY(s) - y(0)   = sY(s) + 2\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $f(t)=2\\sin(2t-\\pi).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\} = e^{-as} \\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\pi )\\} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{2sin\\,(2\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\pi )\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-3L\\{y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\pi )\\} \\\\ (sY(s)+2)-3Y(s) & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s-3)Y(s)+2 & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s-3)Y(s) & =-2+\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229172 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8979fd62-065d-5783-b1c9-4baf45f041fa" "question-965ae37e-9b0d-55ee-82fd-8e26fc17d04a" "question-847985b8-e342-54d5-a829-10e82cf593d2" "question-305f1606-0562-59f2-b1e4-3359528e4a32"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228947 :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential $e^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+4)$ and a repeated linear factor $s^2,$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)} = \\dfrac{A}{s} + \\dfrac{B}{s^2} + \\dfrac{C}{s+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1 = As(s+4) + B(s+4) + Cs^2.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A, B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1 = A(0) + B(4) + C(0) \\implies B = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n1 = A(0) + B(0) + C(16) \\implies C = \\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of $s^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n0 = A + C \\implies A = -C = -\\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s) = -\\dfrac{1}{16s} + \\dfrac{1}{4s^2} + \\dfrac{1}{16(s+4)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t) = \\mathcal L^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{1}{16s}+\\frac{1}{4{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{16(s+4)}\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{t}{4}+\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the $e^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\ne^{-as}\\cdot F(s)  = \\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1} \\{ e^{-as}F(s) \\} = u(t-a) \\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)[-\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{t-3}{4}+\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4(t-3)}] \\\\  & =u(t-3)(\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4(t-3)}+\\frac{4t-13}{16}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t) = -2e^{-4t} + u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t) = \\dfrac{1}{16}e^{-4(t-3)} + \\dfrac{4t-13}{16}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186317228 :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y={u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi }(t)=u(t-\\pi )$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\pi $ and $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi ).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-\\pi )\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\\} & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡((t+\\pi )-\\pi )\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\\} \\\\ sY(s)+Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{({s}^{2}+1)(s+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186316988 :answer-field/correct "answer-bc993c55-c472-52a8-9bc3-380b2766789d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bc993c55-c472-52a8-9bc3-380b2766789d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc993c55-c472-52a8-9bc3-380b2766789d" :answer/id #uuid "bc993c55-c472-52a8-9bc3-380b2766789d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{e^{-\\pi s}}{(s+1)(s^2+1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-137d2706-a3fe-517c-a8b7-db4dd05fae28" :question/id #uuid "137d2706-a3fe-517c-a8b7-db4dd05fae28" :question/math-academy-id "q-342547" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y={u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /2}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+4}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+4y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-2:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-(-2)=sY(s)+2\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $f(t)=sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{sin\\,(3\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} \\\\ (sY(s)+2)+4Y(s) & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s)+2 & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =-2+\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{2}{s+4}+\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d0d6b55c-5888-5098-8079-b16eed82738c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d0d6b55c-5888-5098-8079-b16eed82738c" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0d6b55c-5888-5098-8079-b16eed82738c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0f96584d-4303-56a1-ab81-33e926b99d35" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0f96584d-4303-56a1-ab81-33e926b99d35"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f96584d-4303-56a1-ab81-33e926b99d35" :answer/id #uuid "0f96584d-4303-56a1-ab81-33e926b99d35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-da40e310-ec0b-5b91-ae08-d59c190a3606" :question/id #uuid "da40e310-ec0b-5b91-ae08-d59c190a3606" :question/math-academy-id "q-342771" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y={u}_{\\pi /3}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /3}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+4y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /3}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /3}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\pi /3}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $f(t)=cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /3)s}\\cdot L\\{cos\\,(2\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/3}\\cdot L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/3}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{s{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /3}(t)cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\} \\\\ sY(s)+4Y(s) & =\\frac{s{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{s{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{s{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-45ab7f5f-7155-55c1-b6f0-ecb774ed5d33"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-45ab7f5f-7155-55c1-b6f0-ecb774ed5d33" :answer-field/id #uuid "45ab7f5f-7155-55c1-b6f0-ecb774ed5d33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5c87e25c-189d-5204-85e6-f4884b92e797" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5c87e25c-189d-5204-85e6-f4884b92e797"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c87e25c-189d-5204-85e6-f4884b92e797" :answer/id #uuid "5c87e25c-189d-5204-85e6-f4884b92e797" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{se^{-\\pi s/3}}{(s^2+4)(s+4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229737 :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y={u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>1$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)={e}^{t-2}.$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed exponential forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-2)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L\\{{e}^{(t+2)-2}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L\\{{e}^{t}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\} \\\\ sY(s)+Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s-1)(s+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-e21a8112-0441-5b7a-9e9e-a86738fe8b02" :question/id #uuid "e21a8112-0441-5b7a-9e9e-a86738fe8b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-342542" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=4{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>2$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}-2y\\} & =L\\{4{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-2L\\{y\\} & =L\\{4{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n4{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=3$ and $f(t)=4{e}^{t-3}.$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed exponential forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{4u(t-3)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\\} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L\\{4{e}^{(t+3)-3}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L\\{4{e}^{t}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{4}{s-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{-3s}}{s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-2L\\{y\\} & =L\\{4{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\\} \\\\ sY(s)-2Y(s) & =\\frac{4{e}^{-3s}}{s-1} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{4{e}^{-3s}}{s-1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{4{e}^{-3s}}{(s-1)(s-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-237b2bf3-3e95-50eb-9a6d-6377b31e46da"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-237b2bf3-3e95-50eb-9a6d-6377b31e46da" :answer-field/id #uuid "237b2bf3-3e95-50eb-9a6d-6377b31e46da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-808a3dc9-595d-505a-9f10-17cbb9e73f54" :answer-field/choices ["answer-808a3dc9-595d-505a-9f10-17cbb9e73f54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-808a3dc9-595d-505a-9f10-17cbb9e73f54" :answer/id #uuid "808a3dc9-595d-505a-9f10-17cbb9e73f54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4e^{-3s}}{(s-1)(s-2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8979fd62-065d-5783-b1c9-4baf45f041fa" :question/id #uuid "8979fd62-065d-5783-b1c9-4baf45f041fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-342811" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y={u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{-5(t-3)}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{(s-4)(s+5)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the initial value problem, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s).$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first ignore the exponential term ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to a time shift) and focus on the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s-4)(s+5)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s-4)(s+5)}=\\frac{A}{s-4}+\\frac{B}{s+5}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+5)+B(s-4).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(9)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-9)\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{1}{s-4}-\\frac{1}{s+5}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{9}({L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+5}\\}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{-5t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4(t-3)}-{e}^{-5(t-3)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=\\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4(t-3)}-{e}^{-5(t-3)}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d7838ff7-32b3-503c-9d51-a06dd6fe14d2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d7838ff7-32b3-503c-9d51-a06dd6fe14d2" :answer-field/id #uuid "d7838ff7-32b3-503c-9d51-a06dd6fe14d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9703e3d9-bfb7-5f17-8106-1258f1821bbb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c72baafc-5ab2-555e-85d3-8cbb91b8e24a" "answer-32046168-bf15-539e-a0bf-0354d06e8d28" "answer-9703e3d9-bfb7-5f17-8106-1258f1821bbb" "answer-d81c41ca-a50b-5956-9a30-f2989bbe8749" "answer-1e4e0b48-8667-556e-81ec-ea459847a89c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c72baafc-5ab2-555e-85d3-8cbb91b8e24a" :answer/id #uuid "c72baafc-5ab2-555e-85d3-8cbb91b8e24a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4(t-3)}-{e}^{-5(t-3)})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32046168-bf15-539e-a0bf-0354d06e8d28" :answer/id #uuid "32046168-bf15-539e-a0bf-0354d06e8d28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4(t-3)}+{e}^{-5(t-3)})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9703e3d9-bfb7-5f17-8106-1258f1821bbb" :answer/id #uuid "9703e3d9-bfb7-5f17-8106-1258f1821bbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4(t-3)}-{e}^{-5(t-3)})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d81c41ca-a50b-5956-9a30-f2989bbe8749" :answer/id #uuid "d81c41ca-a50b-5956-9a30-f2989bbe8749" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{-5t})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e4e0b48-8667-556e-81ec-ea459847a89c" :answer/id #uuid "1e4e0b48-8667-556e-81ec-ea459847a89c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{4(t-3)}-{e}^{-5(t-3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-965ae37e-9b0d-55ee-82fd-8e26fc17d04a" :question/id #uuid "965ae37e-9b0d-55ee-82fd-8e26fc17d04a" :question/math-academy-id "q-342810" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3y={u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{-4(t-2)}\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s+3)(s+4)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-2s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s+3)(s+4)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s+3)(s+4)}=\\frac{A}{s+3}+\\frac{B}{s+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+4)+B(s+3).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(1)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=1.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-1)\\,⟹\\,B=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s+4}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s+4}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3t}-{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-2s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2)f(t-2) & =u(t-2)[{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3c95e702-817d-5000-89eb-1d91beb9276d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3c95e702-817d-5000-89eb-1d91beb9276d" :answer-field/id #uuid "3c95e702-817d-5000-89eb-1d91beb9276d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3a5bb7a9-1a25-50f0-8b9f-6a548165a532" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f7e26abd-98f0-5b0a-bf6e-57ca430a9667" "answer-0126ee72-ff17-5f29-8f3a-2ad3ee6df269" "answer-ee49073d-5c8b-5dfd-ba92-d96d34e4671d" "answer-3a5bb7a9-1a25-50f0-8b9f-6a548165a532" "answer-46be3b8a-e417-5b1c-bcce-748fd498356f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7e26abd-98f0-5b0a-bf6e-57ca430a9667" :answer/id #uuid "f7e26abd-98f0-5b0a-bf6e-57ca430a9667" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{e}^{-3(t-2)}+{e}^{-4(t-2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0126ee72-ff17-5f29-8f3a-2ad3ee6df269" :answer/id #uuid "0126ee72-ff17-5f29-8f3a-2ad3ee6df269" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee49073d-5c8b-5dfd-ba92-d96d34e4671d" :answer/id #uuid "ee49073d-5c8b-5dfd-ba92-d96d34e4671d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-3(t-1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a5bb7a9-1a25-50f0-8b9f-6a548165a532" :answer/id #uuid "3a5bb7a9-1a25-50f0-8b9f-6a548165a532" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46be3b8a-e417-5b1c-bcce-748fd498356f" :answer/id #uuid "46be3b8a-e417-5b1c-bcce-748fd498356f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-3t}-{e}^{-4t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-847985b8-e342-54d5-a829-10e82cf593d2" :question/id #uuid "847985b8-e342-54d5-a829-10e82cf593d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-342788" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y={u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(2t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /2}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+1)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{5}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$ \n\nHint: You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the initial value problem, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s).$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first ignore the exponential term ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ (which corresponds to a time shift) and focus on the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{2}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+1)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+1)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+4),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+1)}=\\frac{A}{s+1}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n2=A({s}^{2}+4)+(Bs+C)(s+1).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n2=A((-1)^{2}+4)+0\\,⟹\\,5A=2\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n2=4A+C\\,⟹\\,C=2-\\frac{8}{5}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{-s+1}{{s}^{2}+4})=\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{2}{5}({L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+1}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}\\}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{-t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ term in $Y(s).$ By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}[{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}-cos\\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\frac{1}{2}sin\\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-\\pi /2))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-\\pi /2))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}[{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})(2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=\\frac{1}{5}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-22643dcd-e934-597d-ad21-1b1e44a5206d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-22643dcd-e934-597d-ad21-1b1e44a5206d" :answer-field/id #uuid "22643dcd-e934-597d-ad21-1b1e44a5206d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4a57e159-8b9f-5e50-a811-ef9584e66726" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9f3a6c29-f691-50d0-a36e-fb4594dc00b0" "answer-fe7c7845-1582-524e-ac10-277c2817e843" "answer-4a57e159-8b9f-5e50-a811-ef9584e66726" "answer-f6208ede-9d9d-5811-9f98-c396b7811777" "answer-53bc9662-bdf2-5c5a-b51c-9e4907f321d6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f3a6c29-f691-50d0-a36e-fb4594dc00b0" :answer/id #uuid "9f3a6c29-f691-50d0-a36e-fb4594dc00b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe7c7845-1582-524e-ac10-277c2817e843" :answer/id #uuid "fe7c7845-1582-524e-ac10-277c2817e843" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a57e159-8b9f-5e50-a811-ef9584e66726" :answer/id #uuid "4a57e159-8b9f-5e50-a811-ef9584e66726" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6208ede-9d9d-5811-9f98-c396b7811777" :answer/id #uuid "f6208ede-9d9d-5811-9f98-c396b7811777" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53bc9662-bdf2-5c5a-b51c-9e4907f321d6" :answer/id #uuid "53bc9662-bdf2-5c5a-b51c-9e4907f321d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186317324 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3y={u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+3)$ and a repeated linear factor ${s}^{2},$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{s+3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=As(s+3)+B(s+3)+C{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(3)+C(0)\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(0)+C(9)\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+C\\,⟹\\,A=-C=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)(-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t-3}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}) \\\\  & =u(t-3)(\\frac{3t-10}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-305f1606-0562-59f2-b1e4-3359528e4a32" :question/id #uuid "305f1606-0562-59f2-b1e4-3359528e4a32" :question/math-academy-id "q-342786" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-3y={u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)\\,,\\,y(0)=5,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{5}{s-3}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{2}(s-3)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=5{e}^{3t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-2s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s-3)$ and a repeated linear factor ${s}^{2},$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s-3)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{s-3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=As(s-3)+B(s-3)+C{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-3)+C(0)\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(0)+C(9)\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+C\\,⟹\\,A=-C=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{1}{9s}-\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s-3)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{1}{9s}-\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s-3)}\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}-\\frac{t}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-2s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2)f(t-2) & =u(t-2)(-\\frac{1}{9}-\\frac{t-2}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}) \\\\  & =u(t-2)(-\\frac{3t-5}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=5{e}^{3t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}-\\frac{3t-5}{9}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6cbf215b-97cc-524e-b504-f280dea50225"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6cbf215b-97cc-524e-b504-f280dea50225" :answer-field/id #uuid "6cbf215b-97cc-524e-b504-f280dea50225" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bacb037c-ce80-5974-9d4b-3e4d832343d6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5f13f1d2-9669-574a-b992-547d64c416c7" "answer-aa54b773-38b6-5c26-897c-a183baeabac4" "answer-58f86b27-aaef-5734-b574-cf851cfc63b7" "answer-42a492d9-cf7a-517a-a388-35a26f6d769e" "answer-bacb037c-ce80-5974-9d4b-3e4d832343d6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f13f1d2-9669-574a-b992-547d64c416c7" :answer/id #uuid "5f13f1d2-9669-574a-b992-547d64c416c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}+\\frac{5-3t}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa54b773-38b6-5c26-897c-a183baeabac4" :answer/id #uuid "aa54b773-38b6-5c26-897c-a183baeabac4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}-\\frac{7-3t}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58f86b27-aaef-5734-b574-cf851cfc63b7" :answer/id #uuid "58f86b27-aaef-5734-b574-cf851cfc63b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}+\\frac{3t-5}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42a492d9-cf7a-517a-a388-35a26f6d769e" :answer/id #uuid "42a492d9-cf7a-517a-a388-35a26f6d769e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}+\\frac{7-3t}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bacb037c-ce80-5974-9d4b-3e4d832343d6" :answer/id #uuid "bacb037c-ce80-5974-9d4b-3e4d832343d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}-\\frac{3t-5}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186230233 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we will use Laplace transforms to handle **time-delayed forcing**, where the forcing function turns on at some time $t=a>0$.\n\nWe represent this “turning on” using the **unit step function** $u_a(t)=u(t-a)$.\n\nTo see what this looks like, consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' - 4y = u_2(t)\\cdot e^{t-2}, \\qquad y(0)=0.\n$$\n\nHere, the forcing is $0$ for $t<2$ and becomes $e^{t-2}$ for $t\\ge 2$.\n\nTo find the Laplace transform of the forcing term, we note that\n\n$$\nu_2(t)\\cdot e^{t-2}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)=e^{t-2}.$\n\nNow, recall that the **second shifting theorem** (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\} = e^{-as} \\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-2)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L\\{{e}^{(t+2)-2}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L\\{{e}^{t}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter this step, we proceed as usual: we take the Laplace transform of the entire differential equation, solve for $Y(s)=\\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$, and then (when needed) take $\\mathcal L^{-1}$ to recover $y(t)$." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186230231 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' - 4y = u_2(t)\\cdot e^{t-2}, \\qquad y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $u_2(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nFirst, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the linearity properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}-4y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\mathcal L\\{y'\\},$ we use the derivative formula and the initial condition $y(0)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{y'\\} = sY(s) - y(0) = sY(s).\n$$\n\nTo compute the right-hand side, we note that\n\n$$\nu_2(t)\\cdot e^{t-2}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)=e^{t-2}.$\n\nNow, recall that the **second shifting theorem** (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}=e^{-as}\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-2)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L\\{{e}^{(t+2)-2}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L\\{{e}^{t}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation and solve for $Y(s){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sY(s)-4Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ (s-4)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s-1)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s)$ will give us the solution to our initial value problem." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186230240 :tutorial/content "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' + 3y = u_\\pi(t)\\cdot \\cos(t-\\pi)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=2,\n$$\n\nwhere $u_\\pi(t)=u(t-\\pi)$ is the unit step function.\n\nAssume we are given that the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s) = \\dfrac{2}{s+3} + \\dfrac{s\\,e^{-\\pi s}}{(s^2+1)(s+3)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t) = 2e^{-3t} + \\dfrac{1}{10} u(t-\\pi)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find the function $g(t).$ To do this, we'll need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the second term in $Y(s).$ We can use the following standard results:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad \n\\mathcal L\\{\\cos\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad\ns > 0.\n$$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential $e^{-\\pi s}$ and identify its rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{s}{(s^2+1)(s+3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll find the inverse transform in three steps.\n\n**Step 1. Partial fraction decomposition**\n\nTo handle the rational function $F(s),$ we use *partial fraction decomposition*. Since the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+3)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $(s^2+1),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{s}{(s^2+1)(s+3)} = \\dfrac{A}{s+3} + \\dfrac{Bs+C}{s^2+1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns = A(s^2+1) + (Bs+C)(s+3).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A, B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n-3 = A\\bigl((-3)^2+1\\bigr) + 0 \\implies 10A = -3 \\implies A = -\\dfrac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of $s^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n0 = A + B \\implies B = -A = \\dfrac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n1 = 3B + C \\implies 1 = 3\\!\\left(\\dfrac{3}{10}\\right) + C \\implies C = \\dfrac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)= -\\dfrac{3}{10}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{s+3} + \\dfrac{3}{10}\\cdot\\dfrac{s}{s^2+1} + \\dfrac{1}{10}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{s^2+1}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2. Finding the inverse of the base function**\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t) = \\mathcal L^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3}+\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{10}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(-3{e}^{-3t}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3. Applying the time-shifting property**\n\nFinally, we use the *second shifting theorem* to handle the $e^{-\\pi s}$ factor. The theorem states:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1} \\{ e^{-as}F(s) \\} = u(t-a) \\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\pi.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\pi):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-\\pi )f(t-\\pi ) & =u(t-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[-3{e}^{-3(t-\\pi )}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\pi )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}u(t-\\pi )\\cdot [-3{e}^{-3(t-\\pi )}+3(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $\\cos(t-\\pi)=-\\cos t$ and $\\sin(t-\\pi)=-\\sin t,$ we simplify:\n\n$$\nu(t-\\pi)f(t-\\pi) = \\dfrac{1}{10}u(t-\\pi)\\cdot \\left(-3e^{-3(t-\\pi)} - 3\\cos t - \\sin t\\right).\n$$\n\n**Conclusion: Identifying g(t)**\n\nThis result is the inverse transform of the second term in $Y(s).$ The problem statement gives the complete solution in the form\n\n$$\ny(t) = 2e^{-3t} + \\dfrac{1}{10} u(t-\\pi)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nThe first term, $2e^{-3t},$ is the inverse transform of $\\dfrac{2}{s+3}.$ By comparing our calculated term with the form above, we can identify **$g(t)$**:\n\n$$\ng(t)= -3e^{-3(t-\\pi)} - 3\\cos t - \\sin t.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229269 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "activity-6497987c-07cf-5a53-aba2-92075ada7bf1" :activity/id #uuid "6497987c-07cf-5a53-aba2-92075ada7bf1" :activity/title "Matrix Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048720 :activity/course 17592186046669 :activity/steps ["step-bb7a4a6d-71f1-5525-a73c-4b6460efbd23" "step-01736ca0-0aab-5201-a60f-25493a6b2fd7" "step-98382843-44b7-53a8-b1a3-61c2c6739e6c" "step-72a96283-220e-5289-b060-a3483006b0f8" "step-6490f6a7-a2fa-5388-aee9-2fd4430c4a1f"] :activity/first-step "step-bb7a4a6d-71f1-5525-a73c-4b6460efbd23" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-bb7a4a6d-71f1-5525-a73c-4b6460efbd23" :step/id #uuid "bb7a4a6d-71f1-5525-a73c-4b6460efbd23" :step/content 17592186363586 :step/next "step-01736ca0-0aab-5201-a60f-25493a6b2fd7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-01736ca0-0aab-5201-a60f-25493a6b2fd7" :step/id #uuid "01736ca0-0aab-5201-a60f-25493a6b2fd7" :step/content 17592186363595 :step/next "step-98382843-44b7-53a8-b1a3-61c2c6739e6c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-98382843-44b7-53a8-b1a3-61c2c6739e6c" :step/id #uuid "98382843-44b7-53a8-b1a3-61c2c6739e6c" :step/content 17592186292559 :step/next "step-72a96283-220e-5289-b060-a3483006b0f8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-72a96283-220e-5289-b060-a3483006b0f8" :step/id #uuid "72a96283-220e-5289-b060-a3483006b0f8" :step/content 17592186363647 :step/next "step-6490f6a7-a2fa-5388-aee9-2fd4430c4a1f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6490f6a7-a2fa-5388-aee9-2fd4430c4a1f" :step/id #uuid "6490f6a7-a2fa-5388-aee9-2fd4430c4a1f" :step/content 17592186377013 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186094285 :answer-field/choices 17592186094288] [:db/retract 17592186094285 :answer-field/choices 17592186094289] [:db/retract 17592186094285 :answer-field/choices 17592186094290] [:db/retract 17592186094285 :answer-field/choices 17592186094286] [:db/retract 17592186094285 :answer-field/choices 17592186094287] [:db/retract 17592186095261 :answer-field/choices 17592186095264] [:db/retract 17592186095261 :answer-field/choices 17592186095265] [:db/retract 17592186095261 :answer-field/choices 17592186095266] [:db/retract 17592186095261 :answer-field/choices 17592186095262] [:db/retract 17592186095261 :answer-field/choices 17592186095263] {:db/id 17592186096754 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5fe8b392-c5aa-5b4a-8c32-4dbc47881fb3" "question-848e4810-50fc-59b2-b891-7a6431b7108e" "question-d0d0c8bc-c21f-597b-9878-4cb5aff16c4d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096559 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of a cylinder given that the circumference of the base is $10\\pi \\,\\textrm{m}$ and the ratio of its height to its radius is $\\dfrac{4}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the radius $r$ using the formula $C=2\\pi r,$ where $C$ is the circumference, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi r \\\\ 10\\pi  & =2\\pi r \\\\ r & =5\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the height $h,$ we use the fact that the ratio of the height to the radius is $\\dfrac{4}{5} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{h}{r} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{h}{5} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ h & =4\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2h .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $r = 5\\,\\textrm{m}$ and $h = 4\\,\\textrm{m}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\nV = \\pi(5)^2(4) =  100\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^3 .\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096753 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-32c3dd6f-214e-5de8-b074-37738b30996f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096558 :question/problem "Calculate the radius $r$ of the cylinder below given that its volume is $200\\pi \\,\\textrm{mm}^3$ and its height is $2\\,\\textrm{mm}.$\n\n![](images/d0/d0450b9c0f3ab8b6f5f0cf09c4ca21dac75d0077fe8b44bf740eb4dc198decfe.png)" :question/worked-solution "The volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2h.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $V = 200\\pi \\,\\textrm{mm}^3$ and $h = 2\\,\\textrm{mm}$ into the formula and solving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\ 200\\pi  & =\\pi {r}^{2}(2) \\\\ 2\\pi {r}^{2} & =200\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =100 \\\\ r & =10\\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096755 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-30ca3ff5-4f8e-556f-a019-237ca2e9ceb2" "question-305fc84c-d647-5a56-b2b2-8cf846cd7929"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096560 :question/problem "Find an expression for the height $h$ of a cylinder in terms of its volume $V$ given that the ratio of the radius of the base to the height of the cylinder is $4.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that the ratio of the radius $r$ of the base to the height $h$ is $4,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to express $h$ in terms of $V.$ Therefore, we need to isolate $r$ in the above equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =4 \\\\ r & =4h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h.\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting $r = 4h$ into the formula and solving for $h,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\ V & =\\pi {(4h)}^{2}h \\\\ V & =16\\pi {h}^{3} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =\\frac{V}{16\\pi } \\\\ h & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{V}{16\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-5fe8b392-c5aa-5b4a-8c32-4dbc47881fb3" :question/id #uuid "5fe8b392-c5aa-5b4a-8c32-4dbc47881fb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-13283" :question/problem "![](images/59/595066ea08080810e96c278b54c50f1da30eb155b2b36a29769591d89549a9fd.png)\n\nCalculate the volume of a cylinder given that its diameter is $34\\,\\text{m}$ and its height is $11\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $r=\\frac{34}{2}=17\\,\\text{m}$ and $h=11\\,\\text{m}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\nV=\\pi (17)^{2}(11)=3\\,179\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aa08f2fc-6ed2-57c0-922c-6af8760f784f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aa08f2fc-6ed2-57c0-922c-6af8760f784f" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa08f2fc-6ed2-57c0-922c-6af8760f784f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-acb4c4b1-ea2f-5878-b306-ac1da0914a2c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b66674f5-3b6d-531c-93fa-993a087467b9" "answer-aeab705a-5ccc-59f4-a913-914598ebafaa" "answer-acb4c4b1-ea2f-5878-b306-ac1da0914a2c" "answer-037b31bf-050f-584f-b4cb-33d78910a084" "answer-42407f91-2c20-5182-9196-72ea3f6137fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b66674f5-3b6d-531c-93fa-993a087467b9" :answer/id #uuid "b66674f5-3b6d-531c-93fa-993a087467b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,616\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aeab705a-5ccc-59f4-a913-914598ebafaa" :answer/id #uuid "aeab705a-5ccc-59f4-a913-914598ebafaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,057\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acb4c4b1-ea2f-5878-b306-ac1da0914a2c" :answer/id #uuid "acb4c4b1-ea2f-5878-b306-ac1da0914a2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,179\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-037b31bf-050f-584f-b4cb-33d78910a084" :answer/id #uuid "037b31bf-050f-584f-b4cb-33d78910a084" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,574\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42407f91-2c20-5182-9196-72ea3f6137fe" :answer/id #uuid "42407f91-2c20-5182-9196-72ea3f6137fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "952\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-848e4810-50fc-59b2-b891-7a6431b7108e" :question/id #uuid "848e4810-50fc-59b2-b891-7a6431b7108e" :question/math-academy-id "q-117870" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of a cylinder given that the circumference of the base is $14\\pi \\,\\text{m}$ and the ratio of its height to its radius is $\\frac{1}{49}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the radius $r$ using the formula $C=2\\pi r,$ where $C$ is the circumference, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi r \\\\ 14\\pi  & =2\\pi r \\\\ r & =7\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the height $h$, we use the fact that the ratio of the height to the radius is $\\frac{1}{49}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{h}{r} & =\\frac{1}{49} \\\\ \\frac{h}{7} & =\\frac{1}{49} \\\\ h & =\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $r=7\\,\\text{m}$ and $h=\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{m}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\nV=\\pi \\cdot {7}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}=7\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9944ed2c-3b76-5bfa-8192-ee49f9c9b49e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9944ed2c-3b76-5bfa-8192-ee49f9c9b49e" :answer-field/id #uuid "9944ed2c-3b76-5bfa-8192-ee49f9c9b49e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-be3ab9da-0d37-56a4-a53b-3263659abbe4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-661019cc-b536-5563-b126-8e4a91119d47" "answer-e30df005-616f-5013-b647-c1f3fa257838" "answer-414dfa3d-7db0-5f37-ac64-7f0f59cd5b17" "answer-be3ab9da-0d37-56a4-a53b-3263659abbe4" "answer-45a0e5bc-acd8-50a4-b544-ec302eb8872b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-661019cc-b536-5563-b126-8e4a91119d47" :answer/id #uuid "661019cc-b536-5563-b126-8e4a91119d47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e30df005-616f-5013-b647-c1f3fa257838" :answer/id #uuid "e30df005-616f-5013-b647-c1f3fa257838" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "343\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-414dfa3d-7db0-5f37-ac64-7f0f59cd5b17" :answer/id #uuid "414dfa3d-7db0-5f37-ac64-7f0f59cd5b17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "49\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be3ab9da-0d37-56a4-a53b-3263659abbe4" :answer/id #uuid "be3ab9da-0d37-56a4-a53b-3263659abbe4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45a0e5bc-acd8-50a4-b544-ec302eb8872b" :answer/id #uuid "45a0e5bc-acd8-50a4-b544-ec302eb8872b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097052 :question/problem "![](images/68/68f26a479c5cc1efa978bf6a6390023c07b4215fd7d7d01eb834900d9be696cc.png)\n\nCalculate the volume of a cylinder given that its diameter is $10\\,\\text{in}$ and its height is $8\\,\\text{in}.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $r=\\frac{10}{2}=5\\,\\text{in}$ and $h=8\\,\\text{in}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\nV=\\pi (5)^{2}(8)=200\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186094285 :answer-field/correct "answer-31b17959-4147-5059-a372-6e3f2b81dc48" :answer-field/choices ["answer-61f153d8-fad3-54f5-b55b-5f79a05485cd" "answer-31b17959-4147-5059-a372-6e3f2b81dc48" "answer-c04b462b-927e-5165-81a9-cf451d1c4151" "answer-eb2d3cc3-f256-5336-a395-1c4d057ec83b" "answer-e760a2b4-156c-5ff3-abf4-8954795ae971"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61f153d8-fad3-54f5-b55b-5f79a05485cd" :answer/id #uuid "61f153d8-fad3-54f5-b55b-5f79a05485cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "130\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31b17959-4147-5059-a372-6e3f2b81dc48" :answer/id #uuid "31b17959-4147-5059-a372-6e3f2b81dc48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "200\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c04b462b-927e-5165-81a9-cf451d1c4151" :answer/id #uuid "c04b462b-927e-5165-81a9-cf451d1c4151" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb2d3cc3-f256-5336-a395-1c4d057ec83b" :answer/id #uuid "eb2d3cc3-f256-5336-a395-1c4d057ec83b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e760a2b4-156c-5ff3-abf4-8954795ae971" :answer/id #uuid "e760a2b4-156c-5ff3-abf4-8954795ae971" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "80\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d0d0c8bc-c21f-597b-9878-4cb5aff16c4d" :question/id #uuid "d0d0c8bc-c21f-597b-9878-4cb5aff16c4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-13282" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bc117c302bf4a2963dca000a030d92ae58e6dd13f236826505ca1e732015ba14.png)\n\nCalculate the volume of a cylinder given that its radius is $13\\,\\text{in}$ and its height is $19\\,\\text{in}.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $r=13\\,\\text{in}$ and $h=19\\,\\text{in}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\nV=\\pi (13)^{2}(19)=3\\,211\\pi {\\text{ in}}^{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-de9a3def-d070-54d7-bbf2-c59c7db8f8be"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-de9a3def-d070-54d7-bbf2-c59c7db8f8be" :answer-field/id #uuid "de9a3def-d070-54d7-bbf2-c59c7db8f8be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8e07b930-8463-5659-906b-a56157d2f4b2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cd6696cb-a4b0-587b-87ab-f24acbab5c35" "answer-8e07b930-8463-5659-906b-a56157d2f4b2" "answer-194ad1a2-fdd8-561f-9e4f-0e21e0e6177e" "answer-4a18eb32-1ca8-5373-9bcb-cf3f0d55898d" "answer-1cdcbe33-8e94-5992-8450-f26f66113bfe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd6696cb-a4b0-587b-87ab-f24acbab5c35" :answer/id #uuid "cd6696cb-a4b0-587b-87ab-f24acbab5c35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,016\\pi {\\text{ in}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e07b930-8463-5659-906b-a56157d2f4b2" :answer/id #uuid "8e07b930-8463-5659-906b-a56157d2f4b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,211\\pi {\\text{ in}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-194ad1a2-fdd8-561f-9e4f-0e21e0e6177e" :answer/id #uuid "194ad1a2-fdd8-561f-9e4f-0e21e0e6177e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "494\\pi {\\text{ in}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a18eb32-1ca8-5373-9bcb-cf3f0d55898d" :answer/id #uuid "4a18eb32-1ca8-5373-9bcb-cf3f0d55898d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,693\\pi {\\text{ in}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1cdcbe33-8e94-5992-8450-f26f66113bfe" :answer/id #uuid "1cdcbe33-8e94-5992-8450-f26f66113bfe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "832{\\text{ in}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-32c3dd6f-214e-5de8-b074-37738b30996f" :question/id #uuid "32c3dd6f-214e-5de8-b074-37738b30996f" :question/math-academy-id "q-32475" :question/problem "Calculate the height $h$ of a cylinder given that its volume is $16\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ and the ratio of its height to its radius is $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that  the ratio of the height $h$ to the radius of the base $r$ is $2,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{h}{r} & =2 \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $V=16\\pi {\\text{ cm}}^{3}$ and $r=\\frac{h}{2}$ into the formula, we find the value of $h:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\ 16\\pi  & =\\pi {(\\frac{h}{2})}^{2}\\cdot h \\\\ 16 & =\\frac{{h}^{3}}{4} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =64 \\\\ h & =4\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7eef6173-ab6c-5870-82d9-2a456dd3ae17"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7eef6173-ab6c-5870-82d9-2a456dd3ae17" :answer-field/id #uuid "7eef6173-ab6c-5870-82d9-2a456dd3ae17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d4225406-8293-59b3-af72-40ed3480b4c5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-471c5dea-7288-5a56-958f-067580d15baa" "answer-2e0a5b34-4019-5f4a-8375-4303ad8f7b4c" "answer-d4225406-8293-59b3-af72-40ed3480b4c5" "answer-a93496e4-97c1-5afd-84fd-e7f7ad97ced5" "answer-faba3d5c-7a0d-5929-a968-a619158547ac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-471c5dea-7288-5a56-958f-067580d15baa" :answer/id #uuid "471c5dea-7288-5a56-958f-067580d15baa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "h=2\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e0a5b34-4019-5f4a-8375-4303ad8f7b4c" :answer/id #uuid "2e0a5b34-4019-5f4a-8375-4303ad8f7b4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "h=2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4225406-8293-59b3-af72-40ed3480b4c5" :answer/id #uuid "d4225406-8293-59b3-af72-40ed3480b4c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "h=4\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a93496e4-97c1-5afd-84fd-e7f7ad97ced5" :answer/id #uuid "a93496e4-97c1-5afd-84fd-e7f7ad97ced5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "h=4\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-faba3d5c-7a0d-5929-a968-a619158547ac" :answer/id #uuid "faba3d5c-7a0d-5929-a968-a619158547ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "h=6\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097222 :question/problem "Calculate the circumference of the base of a cylinder given that its volume is $1\\,536\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ and the ratio of its radius to its height is $\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that the ratio of the radius $r$ of the base to the height $h$ is $\\frac{1}{3},$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ h & =3r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $V=1\\,536\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ and $h=3r$ into the formula, we can find the value of $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\ 1\\,536\\pi  & =\\pi {r}^{2}(3r) \\\\ 1\\,536 & =3{r}^{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =512 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{512} \\\\ r & =8\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the formula for the circumference of the base is $C=2\\pi r.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nC=16\\pi \\,\\text{cm}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186095261 :answer-field/correct "answer-eea29e9a-ab4d-5b6c-bfa4-29977d690c97" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2c703f63-eaee-52fe-8bae-7f463f01eb55" "answer-eea29e9a-ab4d-5b6c-bfa4-29977d690c97" "answer-19f9fe2a-0a4d-5175-a120-13f498a8192d" "answer-cb423bc8-8cca-5c73-bda7-b3136d28efe0" "answer-a75cf625-d26e-5229-ae52-7c80f610b6dd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c703f63-eaee-52fe-8bae-7f463f01eb55" :answer/id #uuid "2c703f63-eaee-52fe-8bae-7f463f01eb55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\pi \\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eea29e9a-ab4d-5b6c-bfa4-29977d690c97" :answer/id #uuid "eea29e9a-ab4d-5b6c-bfa4-29977d690c97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16\\pi \\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19f9fe2a-0a4d-5175-a120-13f498a8192d" :answer/id #uuid "19f9fe2a-0a4d-5175-a120-13f498a8192d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20\\pi \\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb423bc8-8cca-5c73-bda7-b3136d28efe0" :answer/id #uuid "cb423bc8-8cca-5c73-bda7-b3136d28efe0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32\\pi \\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a75cf625-d26e-5229-ae52-7c80f610b6dd" :answer/id #uuid "a75cf625-d26e-5229-ae52-7c80f610b6dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pi \\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-30ca3ff5-4f8e-556f-a019-237ca2e9ceb2" :question/id #uuid "30ca3ff5-4f8e-556f-a019-237ca2e9ceb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-82232" :question/problem "Find an expression for the volume $V$ of a cylinder in terms of $h$ given that $\\frac{r}{h}=5,$ where $r$ is the radius of the base and $h$ is the height of the cylinder." :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to express $V$ in terms of $h.$ Therefore, we need to isolate $r$ in the above equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =5 \\\\ r & =5h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting $r=5h$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\pi (5h)^{2}\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 25{h}^{2}\\cdot h \\\\  & =25\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c41b396a-9473-5099-880c-97b185b1f2ad"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c41b396a-9473-5099-880c-97b185b1f2ad" :answer-field/id #uuid "c41b396a-9473-5099-880c-97b185b1f2ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e9954c65-5cd7-5942-b40e-b08333573fbb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e9954c65-5cd7-5942-b40e-b08333573fbb" "answer-a9cb207f-f35a-5e63-8c48-197ca08c5649" "answer-0d93f3da-7de8-5fda-8c73-8d15d9a50060" "answer-110a1701-e7bd-59cc-bf23-b909a11121b8" "answer-15516b73-dbd4-571e-8849-4449a9ef906f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9954c65-5cd7-5942-b40e-b08333573fbb" :answer/id #uuid "e9954c65-5cd7-5942-b40e-b08333573fbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "V=25\\pi {h}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9cb207f-f35a-5e63-8c48-197ca08c5649" :answer/id #uuid "a9cb207f-f35a-5e63-8c48-197ca08c5649" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "V=5\\pi h" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d93f3da-7de8-5fda-8c73-8d15d9a50060" :answer/id #uuid "0d93f3da-7de8-5fda-8c73-8d15d9a50060" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "V=5\\pi {h}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-110a1701-e7bd-59cc-bf23-b909a11121b8" :answer/id #uuid "110a1701-e7bd-59cc-bf23-b909a11121b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "V=\\frac{1}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15516b73-dbd4-571e-8849-4449a9ef906f" :answer/id #uuid "15516b73-dbd4-571e-8849-4449a9ef906f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "V=\\frac{1}{5}\\pi h" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-305fc84c-d647-5a56-b2b2-8cf846cd7929" :question/id #uuid "305fc84c-d647-5a56-b2b2-8cf846cd7929" :question/math-academy-id "q-117880" :question/problem "Find an expression for the volume $V$ of a cylinder in terms of its height $h,$ given that the ratio of the radius of the base to the height of the cylinder is $8.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that the ratio of the radius $r$ of the base to the height $h$ is $8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{h}=8.\n$$\n\nOur goal is to express $V$ in terms of $h.$ Therefore, we need to isolate $r$ in the above equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =8 \\\\ r & =8h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting $r=8h$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\pi (8h)^{2}\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 64{h}^{2}\\cdot h \\\\  & =64\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6828f168-8b35-5454-b18c-be99f62bdfeb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6828f168-8b35-5454-b18c-be99f62bdfeb" :answer-field/id #uuid "6828f168-8b35-5454-b18c-be99f62bdfeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dd0c4f57-7a75-5df2-bd6f-aad1b894860c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd0c4f57-7a75-5df2-bd6f-aad1b894860c" "answer-70819995-5f1f-504a-9a2f-796e854157ab" "answer-9c28f259-1765-546e-b571-116f240c5f24" "answer-421a8784-b386-50cc-a2f4-d72aae749d48" "answer-406b3ae1-b26a-5f6c-9899-76946ce7cfe7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd0c4f57-7a75-5df2-bd6f-aad1b894860c" :answer/id #uuid "dd0c4f57-7a75-5df2-bd6f-aad1b894860c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "V=64\\pi {h}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70819995-5f1f-504a-9a2f-796e854157ab" :answer/id #uuid "70819995-5f1f-504a-9a2f-796e854157ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "V=8\\pi h" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c28f259-1765-546e-b571-116f240c5f24" :answer/id #uuid "9c28f259-1765-546e-b571-116f240c5f24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "V=8\\pi {h}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-421a8784-b386-50cc-a2f4-d72aae749d48" :answer/id #uuid "421a8784-b386-50cc-a2f4-d72aae749d48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "V=\\frac{1}{64}\\pi {h}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-406b3ae1-b26a-5f6c-9899-76946ce7cfe7" :answer/id #uuid "406b3ae1-b26a-5f6c-9899-76946ce7cfe7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "V=\\frac{1}{8}\\pi h" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097875 :tutorial/content "The volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2h.\n$$\n\nLet's use this formula to calculate the volume of the cylinder below:\n\n![](images/be/bef69292f30200f32b78a41f9c0b7585fe35da964ce7f35a594f6d127812e790.png)\n\nFor this cylinder, the radius $r = 3\\,\\textrm{cm}$ and the height $h = 5\\,\\textrm{cm}.$ Substituting these values into the formula for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\nV = \\pi (3)^2 (5) = 45\\pi\\,\\textrm{cm}^3.\n$$\n\nWe often express the volume of a cylinder as a multiple of $\\pi.$ However, we can also use a calculator to get a numerical approximation for the volume. This gives\n\n$$\nV\\approx 141.37\\,\\textrm{cm}^3,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097888 :tutorial/content "Recall that the volume $V$ of a cylinder of radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h.\n$$\n\nThis formula is very intuitive, which makes it easier to remember. \n\nNotice that the base of the cylinder is a circle of radius $r.$ Therefore, the area of a circular base is given by \n\n$$\nA = \\pi r^2.\n$$\n\nLet's label this area on a diagram.\n\n![](images/76/76c1c3179f73ee8464c187d539cebb2692b02763167a21a83857126620338154.png)\n\nTherefore, to compute the volume of the cylinder, we simply multiply the area of the base by the height:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =A\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\pi {r}^{2}h\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096912 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186107607 :answer-field/choices 17592186107608] [:db/retract 17592186107607 :answer-field/choices 17592186107609] [:db/retract 17592186107607 :answer-field/choices 17592186107610] [:db/retract 17592186107607 :answer-field/choices 17592186107611] [:db/retract 17592186107607 :answer-field/choices 17592186107612] {:db/id 17592186110659 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fc247476-de74-5773-8d25-47f3b046cb55"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-fc247476-de74-5773-8d25-47f3b046cb55" :question/id #uuid "fc247476-de74-5773-8d25-47f3b046cb55" :question/math-academy-id "q-299" :question/problem "${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4}=\\frac{{3}^{4}}{{2}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6f62a198-63f6-5ace-982e-2254c7cebad3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6f62a198-63f6-5ace-982e-2254c7cebad3" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f62a198-63f6-5ace-982e-2254c7cebad3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1723e9e6-e3d4-5c92-884a-480d4b3096da" :answer-field/choices ["answer-31d7b2f4-f18a-559c-ace0-d916afe65784" "answer-7fc4dfa5-2ae6-59d3-b3af-d745124b5bd1" "answer-1723e9e6-e3d4-5c92-884a-480d4b3096da" "answer-b2772045-ea3b-5d01-b1ee-fe20dc09c641" "answer-3e978287-ff14-526a-8959-0da6c17344b9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31d7b2f4-f18a-559c-ace0-d916afe65784" :answer/id #uuid "31d7b2f4-f18a-559c-ace0-d916afe65784" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fc4dfa5-2ae6-59d3-b3af-d745124b5bd1" :answer/id #uuid "7fc4dfa5-2ae6-59d3-b3af-d745124b5bd1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{4}}{{3}^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1723e9e6-e3d4-5c92-884a-480d4b3096da" :answer/id #uuid "1723e9e6-e3d4-5c92-884a-480d4b3096da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{4}}{{2}^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2772045-ea3b-5d01-b1ee-fe20dc09c641" :answer/id #uuid "b2772045-ea3b-5d01-b1ee-fe20dc09c641" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{2}^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e978287-ff14-526a-8959-0da6c17344b9" :answer/id #uuid "3e978287-ff14-526a-8959-0da6c17344b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{3}^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110979 :question/problem "${(\\frac{-2x}{5})}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{-2x}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{(-2x)^{3}}{{5}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power of product rule and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(-2x)^{3}}{{5}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{(-2)^{3}{x}^{3}}{125} & = \\\\ -\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107607 :answer-field/correct "answer-c046ba94-1c02-5a90-b029-ab253fc5fbdb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d1256010-75cd-58d3-853b-29ed436d57c3" "answer-c046ba94-1c02-5a90-b029-ab253fc5fbdb" "answer-39117031-8bd7-5923-9276-cdc1948c942c" "answer-9b9949b5-30a6-5762-a953-b694484a6125" "answer-d90f2379-cc17-529b-b846-0f95dcc8aa0b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1256010-75cd-58d3-853b-29ed436d57c3" :answer/id #uuid "d1256010-75cd-58d3-853b-29ed436d57c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{125}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c046ba94-1c02-5a90-b029-ab253fc5fbdb" :answer/id #uuid "c046ba94-1c02-5a90-b029-ab253fc5fbdb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{125}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39117031-8bd7-5923-9276-cdc1948c942c" :answer/id #uuid "39117031-8bd7-5923-9276-cdc1948c942c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b9949b5-30a6-5762-a953-b694484a6125" :answer/id #uuid "9b9949b5-30a6-5762-a953-b694484a6125" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{125}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d90f2379-cc17-529b-b846-0f95dcc8aa0b" :answer/id #uuid "d90f2379-cc17-529b-b846-0f95dcc8aa0b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110660 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-22efbe5c-dc42-517a-9c47-dc7951665162"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-22efbe5c-dc42-517a-9c47-dc7951665162" :question/id #uuid "22efbe5c-dc42-517a-9c47-dc7951665162" :question/math-academy-id "q-22530" :question/problem "$\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{3}{v})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{u})}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power of quotient rule and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{3}{v})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{u})}^{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{2}}{{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{1}^{3}}{{u}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{9}{{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{u}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{18}{3{v}^{2}{u}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{{v}^{2}{u}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.dif